  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nWhile cold air advection continues today into Friday morning, expect \nmainly dry conditions as delta-Ts across Lake Superior will be too \nlow to support lake effect rain showers and high pressure begins to \nenter into the U.P. from the west. Nevertheless, it is possible that \na sprinkle or two could be seen over the area today, but chances for \nthese sprinkles remain fairly low (thinking ~25% or less chance). In \naddition, expect cooler temperatures today as highs only get into \nupper 60s to mid 70s even despite only the partly cloudy skies. The \nCAA (cold air advection) is also creating high winds and waves over \nthe eastern half of Lake Superior today. This in turn is creating \ndangerous waves and rip currents along the beaches of Alger and \nMarquette County today. Therefore, swimmers should avoid going into \nLake Superior until late this afternoon from Marquette County \neastward. \n\nExpect mostly clear to partly cloudy skies tonight. Because of the \ngeneral lack of cloud cover, thinking lows will drop a few degrees \nlower than what the NBM is currently putting down. Thus, followed \nthe day-shift's lead and utilized the NBM10th percentile for lows \ntonight, dropping them down into the 30s and 40s across much of the \ninterior areas; the coldest spots in the interior west could even \ndrop down to around 30 degrees, provided no fog forms. Speaking of \nwhich, because of the large drop in temperature, it is possible that \nsome patchy fog forms over the interior west late tonight. However, \nconfidence on this is not high as fog has yet to be seen over the \narea early this morning as of the time of this writing. That being \nsaid, should fog form over the west later this morning, expect the \nfog chances for tonight to subsequently increase. If fog does not \nform late tonight, then some patchy frost could be seen over the \ncolder parts of the interior west. While it wouldn't be widespread \nenough to warrant a Frost Advisory, it may be prudent to keep an eye \non the forecasted lows later today to see if some plants need to be \ncovered. \n\nExpect temperatures to trend from around normal for the end of this \nwork week to above normal and humid by this weekend into early next \nweek. This is due to a high pressure ridge breaking down as it heads \neastward over our area today into Friday. However, a troughing \npattern is expected to set up shop over the western CONUS by this \nweekend, allowing shortwaves to rotate into our region from the \nRockies. Given the convective nature of the rainfall expected from \nthe shortwaves, it doesn't appear that any individual day looks like \na wash-out. That being said, chances are pretty high (30 to 65% \nchance) relatively speaking for rainfall across the area this \nweekend. In addition, a stronger storm or two cannot be completely \nruled out either as 0-6 km bulk shear forecasts range from around 30 \nkts to 45 kts, even though MUCAPEs look to stay well below 1 kJ/kg \nfor the most part. \n\nExpect the warmer than normal temperatures and humid conditions to \ncontinue through early next week as the troughing pattern holds over \nthe western CONUS and high pressure ridging retreats to the \nSoutheastern U.S.. Hopefully we will see enough rainfall this \nweekend through early next week to relieve some drought concerns \nover the area, particularly in the far west where the drought \nconditions are worst.",
